### What is the first non-repeating character problem?

Given a string, find the first character that appears exactly once. If no such character exists, return a sentinel value. The problem has two parts: counting occurrences and preserving order so you know which unique character came first.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class FirstUniqueLinkedHashMap {
    public static Character firstUnique(String s) {
        Map<Character, Integer> freq = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            freq.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        }
        for (Map.Entry<Character, Integer> entry : freq.entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getValue() == 1) return entry.getKey();
        }
        return null;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(firstUnique("settlement")); // s
        System.out.println(firstUnique("aabb"));       // null
    }
}
```

```text
s
null
```

### Why choose LinkedHashMap over HashMap?

A plain `HashMap` does not remember the order in which keys were inserted. If you iterate a `HashMap`, the entries come out in an unpredictable order, so you cannot determine which unique character came first from the map alone.

A `LinkedHashMap` keeps keys in insertion order. Because you insert a character the first time you see it, the map's iteration order is exactly the order characters first appeared in the string. This lets you iterate the map directly and return the first entry with count one.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class OrderComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String input = "banking";

        Map<Character, Integer> linked = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        Map<Character, Integer> plain = new HashMap<>();

        for (char c : input.toCharArray()) {
            linked.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
            plain.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        }

        System.out.println("LinkedHashMap order: " + linked.keySet());
        System.out.println("HashMap order: " + plain.keySet());
    }
}
```

```text
LinkedHashMap order: [b, a, n, k, i, g]
HashMap order: [a, b, g, i, k, n]
```

### How does the HashMap plus string rescan alternative work?

If you prefer a plain HashMap for counting, you can rescan the original string in a second pass and return the first character whose count is one. The string itself provides the correct order.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class HashMapRescan {
    public static Character firstUnique(String s) {
        Map<Character, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) freq.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (freq.get(c) == 1) return c;
        }
        return null;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(firstUnique("transaction")); // r
    }
}
```

```text
r
```

### How does the fixed-array approach work?

For lowercase ASCII input, an `int[26]` array is the fastest option. It avoids all hashing overhead and uses constant space. The trade-off is that it only works for a known, small character set.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class FixedArrayFirstUnique {
    public static int firstUniqueIndex(String s) {
        int[] freq = new int[26];
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) freq[c - 'a']++;
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (freq[s.charAt(i) - 'a'] == 1) return i;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String code = "payments";
        int idx = firstUniqueIndex(code);
        if (idx >= 0) {
            System.out.println("First unique: '" + code.charAt(idx) + "' at index " + idx);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
First unique: 'p' at index 0
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include empty strings, strings where every character repeats, strings where every character is unique, and single-character strings.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class FirstUniqueEdgeCases {
    public static Character firstUnique(String s) {
        Map<Character, Integer> freq = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) freq.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        for (var e : freq.entrySet()) if (e.getValue() == 1) return e.getKey();
        return null;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty: " + firstUnique(""));
        System.out.println("All repeat: " + firstUnique("aabbcc"));
        System.out.println("All unique: " + firstUnique("xyz"));
        System.out.println("Single: " + firstUnique("a"));
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: null
All repeat: null
All unique: x
Single: a
```

### What about the streaming variant?

If characters arrive one at a time and you need the current first unique character at any moment, use a queue of candidates combined with a count map. Each new character updates the count and is enqueued. To answer a query, poll characters from the front of the queue, discarding any whose count has risen above one, until the front is truly unique.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Queue;

public class StreamingUnique {
    private final Map<Character, Integer> count = new HashMap<>();
    private final Queue<Character> queue = new LinkedList<>();

    public void add(char c) {
        count.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        queue.add(c);
    }

    public Character firstUnique() {
        while (!queue.isEmpty() && count.get(queue.peek()) > 1) queue.poll();
        return queue.isEmpty() ? null : queue.peek();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StreamingUnique s = new StreamingUnique();
        for (char c : "aabcb".toCharArray()) {
            s.add(c);
            System.out.println("After '" + c + "': " + s.firstUnique());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
After 'a': a
After 'a': null
After 'b': b
After 'c': b
After 'b': c
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

All three approaches (LinkedHashMap, HashMap + rescan, fixed array) run in O(n) time with two passes. The LinkedHashMap and HashMap approaches use O(k) space for k distinct characters. The fixed-array approach uses O(1) space because the array size is constant at 26 regardless of input length. The streaming variant processes each character in amortised O(1) time.

### What is the banking use case?

At National Bank of India, Rajesh processes compact strings of single-character transaction type codes and surfaces the first one-off code among many repeats. A one-off transaction type can flag an unusual operation for review by Priya. When every code repeats, the system returns a clean empty result and logs the observation.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Optional;

public class TransactionCodeAnalyzer {
    public static Optional<Character> firstOneOffCode(String codes) {
        Map<Character, Integer> freq = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        for (char c : codes.toCharArray()) freq.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        for (var entry : freq.entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getValue() == 1) return Optional.of(entry.getKey());
        }
        return Optional.empty();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String codes = "DDTWWDTP"; // D=Deposit, T=Transfer, W=Withdrawal, P=Payment
        Optional<Character> unique = firstOneOffCode(codes);
        unique.ifPresentOrElse(
            c -> System.out.println("Flag one-off code: " + c),
            () -> System.out.println("No one-off codes found")
        );
    }
}
```

```text
Flag one-off code: P
```
